(2. THE SOUND OF MUSIC) '
() '
(Part I: Evelyn Glennie Listens to Sound without Hearing It) '
() '
(Evelyn Glennie was determined to lead a normal life, in spite of being profoundly) '
(deaf. At the age of eleven, Evelyn Glennie made up her mind that she wanted to be a) '
(musician. Her teacher, Ron Forbes, told her to feel the note of A and to feel the) '
(sound through her body. She learnt to open her mind and body to the sounds around) '
(her.) '
() '
(Evelyn was just eight years old when she first became aware that she was losing her) '
(hearing. By the age of eleven she was profoundly deaf. But this did not prevent her) '
(from pursuing her ambition.) '
() '
(When Evelyn was about to start secondary school, her mother was informed by the) '
(school doctor that she was profoundly deaf and that her employment perspectives were) '
(very poor. Her parents and teachers at the Royal Academy of Music in London) '
(disagreed. She was allowed to join.) '
() '
(She worked with a percussionist, Ron Forbes, who opened up a new world for her. He) '
(banged a large drum twice and told her to listen with her hands rather than her ears.) '
(He told Evelyn to feel the vibrations in her body. After a while, Evelyn began to) '
(detect the change in pitch.) '
() '
(The nerve of hearing in her ear had been damaged beyond repair. Most people would) '
(have given up, but Evelyn was determined. She practised hard and won a scholarship to) '
(the Royal Academy of Music. Today she is a world-famous musician.) '
() '
(Part II: The Shehnai of Bismillah Khan) '
() '
(The sound of the shehnai is considered auspicious and is associated with all good) '
(things in life. Ustad Bismillah Khan is credited with taking this instrument beyond) '
(the threshold of the traditional performance spaces.) '
() '
(The shehnai is a wind musical instrument. It is made of wood and has a double reed at) '
(the top from which air is blown. Its lower end opens out into a funnel shape. There) '
(are between six and nine holes on its body. The shehnai was traditionally played in) '
(temples and on auspicious occasions like royal processions and weddings.) '
() '
ET
endstream
