(3. THE LITTLE GIRL) '
() '
(To the little girl he was a figure to be feared and avoided. Every morning before) '
(going to work her father came into her room and gave her a casual kiss, to which she) '
(responded with 'Goodbye, Father'. And oh, there was a glad sense of relief when she) '
(heard the gate bang behind the tall, stiff figure!) '
() '
(And then one day she saw something remarkable. She saw that her father was just an) '
(ordinary man. Not a god, not a monster, but a man who worked hard and was tired. It) '
(changed everything.) '
() '
(Kezia was the daughter. She was afraid of her father. She would stay in the drawing) '
(room, well away from the study where her father worked in the evenings. She would) '
(tiptoe and speak softly whenever she was made to go near his study.) '
() '
(One day she was asked to make a pin cushion for her father's birthday. Searching for) '
(stuffing she found her father's important speech papers and used them as stuffing.) '
(She was beaten for this mistake.) '
() '
(But during a terrible nightmare, it was her father who held her safely and gently.) '
(She realized then that her father loved her dearly. She snuggled against his warm) '
(chest, and her fear melted away.) '
() '
ET
