(4. A TRULY BEAUTIFUL MIND) '
() '
(Albert Einstein was born on 14 March 1879 in Ulm, Germany. He was slow to learn to) '
(speak as a child. His mother worried that something was wrong with him. But) '
(Einstein's grandmother thought he was thoroughly lovable, and his mother was hopeful) '
(that nothing was wrong.) '
() '
(Albert's playmates thought he was boring. He preferred to play cards alone. His) '
(sister, Maja, said he used to build card houses that stood up to fourteen storeys.) '
() '
(In school, Albert hated the mechanical learning and memorization. He rebelled against) '
(the German way of teaching. He was extraordinarily curious about science. At the age) '
(of twelve he taught himself algebra and Euclidean geometry over a single summer.) '
() '
(At fifteen, his father's business failed and the family moved to Milan, Italy. Albert) '
(stayed back and finished his schooling in Switzerland. In 1900, he graduated from) '
(Zurich Polytechnic, but could not find a job for two years.) '
() '
(In 1902, he got a job at the Patent Office in Berne. In 1905, while still working) '
(there, he published three scientific papers that changed our understanding of nature.) '
(His Special Theory of Relativity introduced the most famous equation in physics:) '
(E=mc .) '
() '
(Albert Einstein won the Nobel Prize in Physics in 1921. He was also a campaigner for) '
(peace. He wrote a letter to Franklin D. Roosevelt urging the United States not to use) '
(atomic weapons. His message was: peace is more important than any political) '
(objective.) '
() '
ET
