(5. THE SNAKE AND THE MIRROR) '
() '
(The doctor recalled the incident. He was a young doctor at the time. He had a poor) '
(practice and lived in a small rented room. The room was small, dirty and smelly. In) '
(the night, the rats used to have a field day. There was no electricity.) '
() '
(One night, the doctor lit a kerosene lamp and sat down to read a book. He had had his) '
(supper. The room was very hot. He was reading a medical book. The sound of a rat or) '
(some other small animal could be heard now and then.) '
() '
(Suddenly the lamp began to flicker. He looked up and saw a fat snake crawling out) '
(from a hole in the wall. The snake slithered along the floor and then coiled itself) '
(on the arm of the chair where the doctor sat. The snake then raised its head and) '
(looked at the mirror, as if it were a wonderful thing to look at itself.) '
() '
(The doctor sat still. Cold sweat poured down. He was very afraid. Then he had an) '
(idea. He slowly stood up and walked out of the room without making a noise. He ran to) '
(his friend's house and told him everything.) '
() '
(When they came back, the snake had gone. The wife of his friend, a jealous woman, had) '
(left her husband's house that night. The snake had coiled itself around her arm as) '
(she was dressing to go out. She ran away screaming, and the snake also disappeared.) '
() '
ET
endstream
